P      10.96.191.234    <none>                                     8082/TCP                              92s\nuser                     ClusterIP      10.110.99.234    <none>                                     8086/TCP                              86s\n', id='a9359cae-052c-48a4-92ae-6d5ffb245d2c', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-87907f9fd35651b0'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='2c5a7657-3e1f-4251-8d85-4b3636895af9'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to monitor and diagnose a hotel reservation application with many microservices. The first step was to get the list of services using kubectl get services in the hotel-reservation namespace. The response showed a bunch of services like frontend, geo, memcached instances, MongoDB services, and others. \n\nNow, the next step is to figure out which of these services might be faulty. Since the user mentioned that some or none of the microservices have faults, I need to check for issues. The initial list gives service names, but I need more details on their health and performance.\n\nLooking at the available tools, get_metrics could be useful here. Prometheus is commonly used for metrics, so querying metrics like error rates, latency, or pod statuses might reveal problems. For example, if a service has high error rates or is unreachable, that\'s a sign of a fault. \n\nAlternatively, get_traces from Jaeger could help identify issues in the service dependencies. If there are traces showing delays or failures in specific services, that could point to the root cause. But first, maybe checking the metrics would give a broader overview. \n\nAnother option is get_dependency_graph to see how services are interconnected. If a service is down, it might affect others. But without knowing which services are problematic, starting with metrics makes sense. \n\nWait, the user also mentioned checking pods and deployments. Maybe using kubectl to get pods and their statuses would help. The exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd can run "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation" to check if any pods are in CrashLoopBackOff or similar states. That\'s a good next step. \n\nBut the user\'s previous step was to get services, not pods. So maybe the next step is to check the pods\' statuses. Let me think. If the services are up but pods are crashing, that\'s a problem. So using kubectl get pods would show if any pods are not running properly. \n\nAlternatively, using get_metrics with specific queries like "up" or "http_requests_total" could indicate if services are responding. But without knowing the exact metrics, it\'s a bit vague. \n\nHmm, the user might need to first check the pod statuses. So the next tool should be exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation". That would list all pods in the namespace and their current status. If any pods are in an error state, that\'s a starting point. \n\nYes, that makes sense. So the tool choice would be exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command to get pods. That\'s the next logical step after listing services.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The previous step listed services, but to identify faulty microservices, I need to check the actual pod statuses and deployments. Running `kubectl get pods` in the `hotel-reservation` namespace will reveal if any pods are in CrashLoopBackOff, Error, or other abnormal states. This is critical for identifying immediate runtime issues. Additionally, checking deployments with `kubectl get deployments` would show replica statuses and rollout health.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 644, 'prompt_tokens': 2576, 'total_tokens': 3220, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc6c-edf2-72f0-84d1-4a25d6f0040d-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2576, 'output_tokens': 644, 'total_tokens': 3220}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='be1d542d-bcbf-400c-b27e-25269037801c'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose a hotel reservation application with many microservices. They first used kubectl get services to list all services in the namespace, which gave a list of services like frontend, geo, jaeger, various MongoDB instances, and others.\n\nNow, the next step is to check the pods and deployments. The user\'s reasoning was to use kubectl get pods to check for any abnormal states like CrashLoopBackOff or Error. Also, checking deployments with kubectl get deployments to see replica statuses and rollout health. \n\nBut wait, the available commands for exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd include "kubectl get pods" and "kubectl get deployments". So, the user should run those commands in the hotel-reservation namespace. However, the user can only make one tool call per step. Which one is more critical here?\n\nSince the user wants to check for faulty microservices, checking the pods first makes sense because pods are the actual running instances. If any pods are in a bad state, that\'s a direct indicator of a problem. So, the first tool call should be kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation. That will show the current status of all pods. If there are any issues, they\'ll be visible here. Then, if needed, they can check deployments next. But for the first step, focusing on pods is logical.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b41baf6e3ada8561', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 314, 'prompt_tokens': 3578, 'total_tokens': 3892, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc6d-0bb5-75c0-b2cd-c60f11629ed7-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b41baf6e3ada8561', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3578, 'output_tokens': 314, 'total_tokens': 3892}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                                      READY   STATUS              RESTARTS      AGE\nconsul-5c696cc4dc-84z46                   1/1     Running             0             2m6s\nfrontend-6df8475df4-kqpb2                 1/1     Running             0             2m5s\ngeo-57c7bd6d5c-ml6hb                      1/1     Running             2 (87s ago)   2m5s\njaeger-5b9775bd94-g255h                   1/1     Running             0             2m2s\nmemcached-profile-6849578b8-85q7g         1/1     Running             0             119s\nmemcached-rate-948594dfd-xjfv5            1/1     Running             0             117s\nmemcached-reserve-68dbb57476-l67t5        1/1     Running             0             110s\nmongodb-geo-8cb7d9f5b-9bzrw               0/1     ContainerCreating   0             40s\nmongodb-profile-775dfd4bd7-jdjrr          1/1     Running             0             118s\nmongodb-rate-bfbcf4587-fjzp4              1/1     Running             0             116s\nmongodb-recommendation-674dddbcdf-z6k6t   1/1     Running             0             114s\nmongodb-reservation-6cdf69dcc-w4xmz       1/1     Running             0             108s\nmongodb-user-6dcf4877-4fdvk               1/1     Running             0             102s\nprofile-69cb98cb67-hdv5p                  1/1     Running             2 (79s ago)   118s\nrate-6fff88f6f5-h2tj7                     1/1     Running             2 (75s ago)   115s\nrecommendation-64754c9c4f-dcj7t           1/1     Running             2 (74s ago)   112s\nreservation-7bb77b596b-2q6rd              1/1     Running             2 (71s ago)   107s\nsearch-5f668579c9-6bjxl                   1/1     Running             0             104s\nuser-6548d868cd-p59c8                     1/1     Running             2 (61s ago)   100s\nwrk2-job-pdgh2                            1/1     Running             0             41s\n', id='3d28a446-a0f3-4171-a846-7f4532776648', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-b41baf6e3ada8561'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='a9944845-275a-4c83-ba97-397b875c3f1e'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose a hotel reservation application with many microservices. So far, they\'ve checked the services and pods. The services list showed some services like frontend, geo, mongodb instances, etc. Then they checked the pods and found that most are running, but a few have restarts. For example, geo has 2 restarts, profile, rate, recommendation, reservation, and user pods also have restarts. But the status is still Running. \n\nWait, the STATUS column shows "Running" even with restarts. So maybe the pods are restarting but then coming back up. But why? Could be configuration issues, dependency problems, or application errors. The next step is to check the deployments to see the replica statuses and rollout history. Because if deployments are not stable, that could indicate issues with the rollout process.\n\nSo the user should check the deployments using kubectl get deployments. That would show the number of replicas, current status, and any rollout errors. If there are deployment issues, like failed rollouts or scaling problems, that could be the root cause. Also, checking the deployment details might show why the pods are restarting. For example, if the deployment has a bad image or configuration, that could cause the pods to crash and restart.\n\nAnother possibility is that some services are dependent on others. For instance, the mongodb-geo pod is in ContainerCreating state. That might be a problem. But the user hasn\'t checked the deployments yet. So first, checking deployments would help understand if there are any issues with the deployment configurations. If deployments are healthy, then the next step would be to look into the specific services that are restarting, maybe check their logs or metrics. But since the user has a tool to get metrics, maybe they can check for errors or high latency. However, the immediate next step is to check deployments to see if there\'s a rollout issue or replica problems.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The pod list shows some pods with restarts (e.g., `geo`, `profile`, `rate`, etc.), but their status is still "Running." To investigate deeper, I need to check the deployments to see if there are rollout issues, replica mismatches, or deployment health problems. This will help identify if the deployment configurations are causing instability. For example, a failed rollout or scaling issue could lead to repeated pod restarts. Running `kubectl get deployments` in the namespace will reveal deployment statuses and potential errors.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 515, 'prompt_tokens': 3454, 'total_tokens': 3969, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc6d-1d1f-79d3-8c2c-22dec0f25ca5-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3454, 'output_tokens': 515, 'total_tokens': 3969}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='0386545d-b94d-4b7b-8b08-035d398c513b'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose issues in a hotel reservation application composed of many microservices. So far, they've used kubectl to list services and pods. The services list showed some Jaeger components and MongoDB instances, while the pods list indicated that most are running, but a few have restarts. The user is now looking into deployments to check for rollout issues or replica problems.\n\nThe next logical step is to check the deployments. By running `kubectl get deployments`, they can see the deployment statuses, replica counts, and any rollout errors. This will help determine if there are issues with how the applications are being deployed or scaled. For example, if a deployment has a rollout that's failed or stuck, that could be the root cause of the restarts observed in some pods. Also, checking the number of replicas and their current status can reveal if there's a mismatch that's causing instability. So, using the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool with the command `kubectl get deployments -n hotel-reservation` makes sense here. This will provide the necessary information to proceed further in diagnosing the problem.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get deployments -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8c4226a48d9948e0', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 264, 'prompt_tokens': 4477, 'total_tokens': 4741, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc6d-35b7-7e92-a6b6-7313e676ac16-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get deployments -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8c4226a48d9948e0', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4477, 'output_tokens': 264, 'total_tokens': 4741}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                     READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE   AGE\nconsul                   1/1     1            1           2m16s\nfrontend                 1/1     1            1           2m15s\ngeo                      1/1     1            1           2m15s\njaeger                   1/1     1            1           2m12s\nmemcached-profile        1/1     1            1           2m10s\nmemcached-rate           1/1     1            1           2m7s\nmemcached-reserve        1/1     1            1           2m1s\nmongodb-geo              0/1     1            0           2m14s\nmongodb-profile          1/1     1            1           2m8s\nmongodb-rate             1/1     1            1           2m6s\nmongodb-recommendation   1/1     1            1           2m4s\nmongodb-reservation      1/1     1            1           119s\nmongodb-user             1/1     1            1           112s\nprofile                  1/1     1            1           2m8s\nrate                     1/1     1            1           2m5s\nrecommendation           1/1     1            1           2m2s\nreservation              1/1     1            1           117s\nsearch                   1/1     1            1           115s\nuser                     1/1     1            1           111s\n', id='171fac05-a120-4410-b5bf-4579333fd0d9', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-8c4226a48d9948e0'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='d7265af9-5ed4-4d88-9d4c-64ccbbdebdc6')]]
2026-03-17 15:32:31 - DEBUG - all.stratus.diagnosis - [Loop 3] Inject tool call prompt (repeated) - base_agent.py:llm_tool_call_prompt_inject_step:85
2026-03-17 15:32:36 - DEBUG - all.stratus.diagnosis - [Loop 3] Tool call - base_agent.py:llm_tool_call_step:102 [Full Prompt = [SystemMessage(content="Monitor and diagnose an application consisting of **MANY** microservices. Some or none of the microservices have faults. Get all the pods and deployments to figure out what kind of services are running in the cluster.  Carefully identify the whether the faults are present and if they are, and identify what is the root cause of the fault.\nStop diagnosis once you've found the root cause of the faults.\nGo as deep as you can into what is causing the issue.\nYour instructions to the tools must be clear and concise. Your queries to tools need to be single turn.\nRemember to check these, and remember this information: ## Workloads (Applications) - **Pod**: The smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes, representing a single instance of a running application. Can contain one or more tightly coupled containers. - **ReplicaSet**: Ensures that a specified number of pod replicas are running at all times. Often managed indirectly through Deployments. - **Deployment**: Manages the deployment and lifecycle of applications. Provides declarative updates for Pods and ReplicaSets. - **StatefulSet**: Manages stateful applications with unique pod identities and stable storage. Used for workloads like databases. - **DaemonSet**: Ensures that a copy of a specific pod runs on every node in the cluster. Useful for node monitoring agents, log collectors, etc. - **Job**: Manages batch processing tasks that are expected to complete successfully. Ensures pods run to completion. - **CronJob**: Schedules jobs to run at specified times or intervals (similar to cron in Linux).\n## Networking - **Service**: Provides a stable network endpoint for accessing a group of pods. Types: ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer, and ExternalName. - **Ingress**: Manages external HTTP(S) access to services in the cluster. Supports routing and load balancing for HTTP(S) traffic. - **NetworkPolicy**: Defines rules for network communication between pods and other entities. Used for security and traffic control.\n## Storage - **PersistentVolume (PV)**: Represents a piece of storage in the cluster, provisioned by an administrator or dynamically. - **PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)**: Represents a request for storage by a user. Binds to a PersistentVolume. - **StorageClass**: Defines different storage tiers or backends for dynamic provisioning of PersistentVolumes. - **ConfigMap**: Stores configuration data as key-value pairs for applications. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive data like passwords, tokens, or keys in an encrypted format.\n## Configuration and Metadata - **Namespace**: Logical partitioning of resources within the cluster for isolation and organization. - **ConfigMap**: Provides non-sensitive configuration data in key-value format. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive configuration data securely. - **ResourceQuota**: Restricts resource usage (e.g., CPU, memory) within a namespace. - **LimitRange**: Enforces minimum and maximum resource limits for containers in a namespace.\n## Cluster Management - **Node**: Represents a worker machine in the cluster (virtual or physical). Runs pods and is managed by the control plane. - **ClusterRole and Role**: Define permissions for resources at the cluster or namespace level. - **ClusterRoleBinding and RoleBinding**: Bind roles to users or groups for authorization. - **ServiceAccount**: Associates processes in pods with permissions for accessing the Kubernetes API.\nAfter you finished, submit the detected faulty components to the orchestrator for evaluation. The submission should be a natural language description of the root cause of the failure.\n", additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='f99d0909-748e-4a14-8188-ccb6daae38a3'), HumanMessage(content="You will be working this application:\nHotel Reservation\nHere are some descriptions about the application:\nA hotel reservation application built with Go and gRPC, providing backend in-memory and persistent databases, a recommender system for hotel recommendations, and a functionality to place reservations.\nIt belongs to this namespace:\nhotel-reservation\nIn each round, there is a thinking stage. In the thinking stage, you are given a list of tools. Think about what you want to call. Return your tool choice and the reasoning behind When choosing the tool, refer to the tool by its name. Then, there is a tool-call stage, where you make a tool_call consistent with your explanation. You can run up to 20 rounds to finish the tasks. If you call submit_tool in tool-call stage, the process will end immediately. If you exceed this limitation, the system will force you to make a submission. You will begin by analyzing the service's state and telemetry with the tools.\n", additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='d85d802e-36c6-4fa9-8a65-2a5a61ef44e2'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Here are all the tools you can use:\ntool name: get_traces\n\ntool description: Get Jaeger traces for a given service in the last n minutes.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service for which to retrieve trace data.\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n\n\ntool name: get_services\n\ntool description: Retrieve the list of service names from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of service names available in Grafana.\n\n\ntool name: get_operations\n\ntool description: Query available operations for a specific service from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service whose operations should be retrieved.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of operation names associated with the specified service.\n\n\ntool name: get_dependency_graph\n\ntool description: Get service dependency graph from Jaeger\'s native dependencies API.\n    Args:\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n    Returns:\n        str: JSON object representing the dependency graph.\n\n\ntool name: get_metrics\n\ntool description: Query real-time metrics data from the Prometheus instance.\n\n    Args:\n        query (str): A Prometheus Query Language (PromQL) expression used to fetch metric values.\n\n    Returns:\n        dict: The raw Prometheus response containing metric results, including timestamps, values, and labels.\n\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\n\ntool description: This is a tool used to execute read-only kubectl commands.\n\n    Args:\n        command (str): The read-only kubectl command you want to execute in a CLI to manage a k8s cluster. It should start with "kubectl".\n        Available Read-only Commands:\n        "kubectl api-resources",\n        "kubectl api-version",\n        "kubectl auth can-i",\n        "kubectl cluster-info",\n        "kubectl describe",\n        "kubectl diff",\n        "kubectl events",\n        "kubectl explain",\n        "kubectl get",\n        "kubectl logs",\n        "kubectl options",\n        "kubectl top",\n        "kubectl version",\n        "kubectl config view",\n        "kubectl config current-context",\n        "kubectl config get",\n        INTERACTIVE COMMANDS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g. "kubectl edit"\n        INTERACTIVE FLAGS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g., "kubectl logs -f"\n\n\ntool name: submit_tool\n\ntool description: The tool to submit benchmark results\n\n    Args:\n        ans (str): the answer you would like to submit to the benchmark. Use natural language to describe the root cause of the failure.\n\n\nChoose a tool from the list and output the tool name. Justify your tool choice. In the next step, you will generate a tool call for this tool', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='6823518f-bc74-4eae-9f92-ff037f65c77f'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's start by understanding the problem. The user is dealing with a hotel reservation application composed of many microservices. The goal is to monitor and diagnose any faults in the system. The first step is to figure out what services are running in the cluster. Since the application is in the namespace 'hotel-reservation', I need to get a list of all the services within that namespace.\n\nLooking at the available tools, the 'get_services' tool from Grafana can retrieve the list of service names. However, the user mentioned that the application uses Kubernetes, so maybe checking the Kubernetes resources would be more direct. The 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd' tool allows running kubectl commands. Using 'kubectl get services' in the 'hotel-reservation' namespace would list all the services. But the user might not have access to the cluster's kubectl, or maybe the Grafana tool is more appropriate here. Wait, the 'get_services' tool is specific to Grafana, which might not list all Kubernetes services but rather services monitored in Grafana. However, the user hasn't specified if Grafana is part of the setup. \n\nAlternatively, using 'get_services' from Grafana might give the list of services that are being monitored, which could be the same as the Kubernetes services. But if the application has many microservices, it's crucial to know all the services first. Since the user hasn't provided specific info, maybe starting with 'get_services' is better. But if that's not available, perhaps using kubectl. However, the 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd' tool can run 'kubectl get services -n hotel-reservation' to list all services in that namespace. That would give the list of services, which is essential for further diagnosis. So, the first step is to get the list of services to identify which ones are part of the application. Therefore, the correct tool here is 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd' with the command 'kubectl get services -n hotel-reservation'.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\nreasoning: To identify all microservices running in the hotel-reservation namespace, I need to first list all services (pods, deployments, etc.) in the cluster. Using kubectl get services in the target namespace will provide the foundational list of services to investigate further. This is the first step in understanding the application's architecture and identifying potential fault points.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 496, 'prompt_tokens': 1500, 'total_tokens': 1996, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc6c-a69f-7313-aeac-e124d3b2f9e1-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1500, 'output_tokens': 496, 'total_tokens': 1996}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='d01dce83-6a39-492f-96c2-e478eb29dfaa'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let me figure out the next step. The user wants me to monitor and diagnose a hotel reservation application with many microservices. The first thing I need to do is get a list of all the services running in the cluster, specifically in the hotel-reservation namespace. \n\nIn the previous step, I chose the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool. The reasoning was that using kubectl get services in the target namespace would provide the list of services. But wait, the user mentioned that the application consists of many microservices, so maybe I should check for pods, deployments, and other resources as well. However, the initial step is to get the services, which are the network endpoints.\n\nWait, the user provided a list of tools. The get_services tool retrieves service names from Grafana, but maybe that's not the same as the Kubernetes services. The exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd can run kubectl commands. So, to get all services in the namespace, the command would be 'kubectl get services -n hotel-reservation'. But the user might also need pods, deployments, etc. However, the first step is to get the services. \n\nWait, the user's initial instruction says to get all pods and deployments. So maybe I should first list all pods and deployments. But the first tool call was to get services. Hmm. Let me check the available commands again. The exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd allows 'kubectl get' commands. So, to get all services, pods, deployments, etc., I need to run separate commands. \n\nBut the user's first step was to get the list of services from Grafana using get_services. However, the get_services tool's description says it retrieves service names from Grafana, which might not be the same as Kubernetes services. The user might be using Grafana for monitoring, so the service names in Grafana could correspond to Kubernetes services. But to be thorough, maybe I should first check the Kubernetes services using kubectl. \n\nWait, the user's initial problem is to monitor and diagnose the application, which has many microservices. The first step is to identify what services are running. So using kubectl to list all services, pods, deployments, etc., in the hotel-reservation namespace would be the right approach. \n\nSo, the first tool call should be to exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command 'kubectl get all -n hotel-reservation' to get an overview of all resources. But the user might need to break it down into services, pods, deployments, etc. Alternatively, run separate commands for each. However, the user's initial instruction says to get all pods and deployments. \n\nWait, the user's first step in the thinking stage was to use get_traces, but the assistant chose exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd. Then, the user's next step is to generate a tool call. So the previous step was to use exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command 'kubectl get services -n hotel-reservation'. But maybe that's not sufficient. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the correct approach is to first list all services in the namespace. So the command would be 'kubectl get services -n hotel-reservation'. But the user might need to check for deployments as well. However, the initial step is to get the list of services. \n\nWait, the user's problem is to identify the microservices, so the first step is to list all services, pods, deployments, etc. So perhaps the correct command is 'kubectl get all -n hotel-reservation' to get all resources. But the available commands in the tool include 'kubectl get', so the command would be 'kubectl get all -n hotel-reservation'. \n\nBut the user's initial answer in the thinking stage was to use exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command 'kubectl get services -n hotel-reservation'. However, the user might need to check for deployments as well. So maybe the next step is to run 'kubectl get deployments -n hotel-reservation' and 'kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation' to get all the deployments and pods. \n\nBut the first step is to get the list of services. So the correct tool call is to use exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command 'kubectl get services -n hotel-reservation'. That would list all services in the namespace. Then, after that, proceed to check deployments and pods. \n\nSo the tool call should be to exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command 'kubectl get services -n hotel-reservation'.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get services -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-87907f9fd35651b0', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 958, 'prompt_tokens': 2487, 'total_tokens': 3445, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc6c-be1d-7681-90c3-05451a26d24c-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get services -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-87907f9fd35651b0', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2487, 'output_tokens': 958, 'total_tokens': 3445}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                     TYPE           CLUSTER-IP       EXTERNAL-IP                                PORT(S)                               AGE\nconsul                   ClusterIP      10.110.214.137   <none>                                     8300/TCP,8400/TCP,8500/TCP,8600/UDP   114s\nfrontend                 ClusterIP      10.111.38.58     <none>                                     5000/TCP                              113s\ngeo                      ClusterIP      10.98.13.89      <none>                                     8083/TCP                              113s\njaeger                   ExternalName   <none>           otel-collector.observe.svc.cluster.local   <none>                                31s\njaeger-agent             ExternalName   <none>           otel-collector.observe.svc.cluster.local   <none>                                31s\njaeger-collector         ExternalName   <none>           otel-collector.observe.svc.cluster.local   <none>                                30s\njaeger-out               NodePort       10.109.0.76      <none>                                     16686:30481/TCP                       110s\njaeger-query             ExternalName   <none>           otel-collector.observe.svc.cluster.local   <none>                                30s\nmemcached-profile        ClusterIP      10.100.53.255    <none>                                     11211/TCP                             107s\nmemcached-rate           ClusterIP      10.107.14.51     <none>                                     11211/TCP                             105s\nmemcached-reserve        ClusterIP      10.97.247.83     <none>                                     11211/TCP                             98s\nmongodb-geo              ClusterIP      10.96.236.129    <none>                                     27017/TCP                             111s\nmongodb-profile          ClusterIP      10.97.205.41     <none>                                     27017/TCP                             106s\nmongodb-rate             ClusterIP      10.107.154.58    <none>                                     27017/TCP                             103s\nmongodb-recommendation   ClusterIP      10.96.45.168     <none>                                     27017/TCP                             102s\nmongodb-reservation      ClusterIP      10.109.9.146     <none>                                     27017/TCP                             96s\nmongodb-user             ClusterIP      10.104.251.32    <none>                                     27017/TCP                             90s\nprofile                  ClusterIP      10.108.211.155   <none>                                     8081/TCP                              105s\nrate                     ClusterIP      10.98.245.51     <none>                                     8084/TCP                              102s\nrecommendation           ClusterIP      10.104.175.96    <none>                                     8085/TCP                              99s\nreservation              ClusterIP      10.103.236.12    <none>                                     8087/TCP                              94s\nsearch                   ClusterIP      10.96.191.234    <none>                                     8082/TCP                              92s\nuser                     ClusterIP      10.110.99.234    <none>                                     8086/TCP                              86s\n', id='a9359cae-052c-48a4-92ae-6d5ffb245d2c', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-87907f9fd35651b0'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='2c5a7657-3e1f-4251-8d85-4b3636895af9'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to monitor and diagnose a hotel reservation application with many microservices. The first step was to get the list of services using kubectl get services in the hotel-reservation namespace. The response showed a bunch of services like frontend, geo, memcached instances, MongoDB services, and others. \n\nNow, the next step is to figure out which of these services might be faulty. Since the user mentioned that some or none of the microservices have faults, I need to check for issues. The initial list gives service names, but I need more details on their health and performance.\n\nLooking at the available tools, get_metrics could be useful here. Prometheus is commonly used for metrics, so querying metrics like error rates, latency, or pod statuses might reveal problems. For example, if a service has high error rates or is unreachable, that\'s a sign of a fault. \n\nAlternatively, get_traces from Jaeger could help identify issues in the service dependencies. If there are traces showing delays or failures in specific services, that could point to the root cause. But first, maybe checking the metrics would give a broader overview. \n\nAnother option is get_dependency_graph to see how services are interconnected. If a service is down, it might affect others. But without knowing which services are problematic, starting with metrics makes sense. \n\nWait, the user also mentioned checking pods and deployments. Maybe using kubectl to get pods and their statuses would help. The exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd can run "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation" to check if any pods are in CrashLoopBackOff or similar states. That\'s a good next step. \n\nBut the user\'s previous step was to get services, not pods. So maybe the next step is to check the pods\' statuses. Let me think. If the services are up but pods are crashing, that\'s a problem. So using kubectl get pods would show if any pods are not running properly. \n\nAlternatively, using get_metrics with specific queries like "up" or "http_requests_total" could indicate if services are responding. But without knowing the exact metrics, it\'s a bit vague. \n\nHmm, the user might need to first check the pod statuses. So the next tool should be exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation". That would list all pods in the namespace and their current status. If any pods are in an error state, that\'s a starting point. \n\nYes, that makes sense. So the tool choice would be exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command to get pods. That\'s the next logical step after listing services.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The previous step listed services, but to identify faulty microservices, I need to check the actual pod statuses and deployments. Running `kubectl get pods` in the `hotel-reservation` namespace will reveal if any pods are in CrashLoopBackOff, Error, or other abnormal states. This is critical for identifying immediate runtime issues. Additionally, checking deployments with `kubectl get deployments` would show replica statuses and rollout health.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 644, 'prompt_tokens': 2576, 'total_tokens': 3220, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc6c-edf2-72f0-84d1-4a25d6f0040d-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2576, 'output_tokens': 644, 'total_tokens': 3220}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='be1d542d-bcbf-400c-b27e-25269037801c'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose a hotel reservation application with many microservices. They first used kubectl get services to list all services in the namespace, which gave a list of services like frontend, geo, jaeger, various MongoDB instances, and others.\n\nNow, the next step is to check the pods and deployments. The user\'s reasoning was to use kubectl get pods to check for any abnormal states like CrashLoopBackOff or Error. Also, checking deployments with kubectl get deployments to see replica statuses and rollout health. \n\nBut wait, the available commands for exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd include "kubectl get pods" and "kubectl get deployments". So, the user should run those commands in the hotel-reservation namespace. However, the user can only make one tool call per step. Which one is more critical here?\n\nSince the user wants to check for faulty microservices, checking the pods first makes sense because pods are the actual running instances. If any pods are in a bad state, that\'s a direct indicator of a problem. So, the first tool call should be kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation. That will show the current status of all pods. If there are any issues, they\'ll be visible here. Then, if needed, they can check deployments next. But for the first step, focusing on pods is logical.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b41baf6e3ada8561', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 314, 'prompt_tokens': 3578, 'total_tokens': 3892, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc6d-0bb5-75c0-b2cd-c60f11629ed7-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pods -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-b41baf6e3ada8561', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3578, 'output_tokens': 314, 'total_tokens': 3892}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                                      READY   STATUS              RESTARTS      AGE\nconsul-5c696cc4dc-84z46                   1/1     Running             0             2m6s\nfrontend-6df8475df4-kqpb2                 1/1     Running             0             2m5s\ngeo-57c7bd6d5c-ml6hb                      1/1     Running             2 (87s ago)   2m5s\njaeger-5b9775bd94-g255h                   1/1     Running             0             2m2s\nmemcached-profile-6849578b8-85q7g         1/1     Running             0             119s\nmemcached-rate-948594dfd-xjfv5            1/1     Running             0             117s\nmemcached-reserve-68dbb57476-l67t5        1/1     Running             0             110s\nmongodb-geo-8cb7d9f5b-9bzrw               0/1     ContainerCreating   0             40s\nmongodb-profile-775dfd4bd7-jdjrr          1/1     Running             0             118s\nmongodb-rate-bfbcf4587-fjzp4              1/1     Running             0             116s\nmongodb-recommendation-674dddbcdf-z6k6t   1/1     Running             0             114s\nmongodb-reservation-6cdf69dcc-w4xmz       1/1     Running             0             108s\nmongodb-user-6dcf4877-4fdvk               1/1     Running             0             102s\nprofile-69cb98cb67-hdv5p                  1/1     Running             2 (79s ago)   118s\nrate-6fff88f6f5-h2tj7                     1/1     Running             2 (75s ago)   115s\nrecommendation-64754c9c4f-dcj7t           1/1     Running             2 (74s ago)   112s\nreservation-7bb77b596b-2q6rd              1/1     Running             2 (71s ago)   107s\nsearch-5f668579c9-6bjxl                   1/1     Running             0             104s\nuser-6548d868cd-p59c8                     1/1     Running             2 (61s ago)   100s\nwrk2-job-pdgh2                            1/1     Running             0             41s\n', id='3d28a446-a0f3-4171-a846-7f4532776648', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-b41baf6e3ada8561'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='a9944845-275a-4c83-ba97-397b875c3f1e'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose a hotel reservation application with many microservices. So far, they\'ve checked the services and pods. The services list showed some services like frontend, geo, mongodb instances, etc. Then they checked the pods and found that most are running, but a few have restarts. For example, geo has 2 restarts, profile, rate, recommendation, reservation, and user pods also have restarts. But the status is still Running. \n\nWait, the STATUS column shows "Running" even with restarts. So maybe the pods are restarting but then coming back up. But why? Could be configuration issues, dependency problems, or application errors. The next step is to check the deployments to see the replica statuses and rollout history. Because if deployments are not stable, that could indicate issues with the rollout process.\n\nSo the user should check the deployments using kubectl get deployments. That would show the number of replicas, current status, and any rollout errors. If there are deployment issues, like failed rollouts or scaling problems, that could be the root cause. Also, checking the deployment details might show why the pods are restarting. For example, if the deployment has a bad image or configuration, that could cause the pods to crash and restart.\n\nAnother possibility is that some services are dependent on others. For instance, the mongodb-geo pod is in ContainerCreating state. That might be a problem. But the user hasn\'t checked the deployments yet. So first, checking deployments would help understand if there are any issues with the deployment configurations. If deployments are healthy, then the next step would be to look into the specific services that are restarting, maybe check their logs or metrics. But since the user has a tool to get metrics, maybe they can check for errors or high latency. However, the immediate next step is to check deployments to see if there\'s a rollout issue or replica problems.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The pod list shows some pods with restarts (e.g., `geo`, `profile`, `rate`, etc.), but their status is still "Running." To investigate deeper, I need to check the deployments to see if there are rollout issues, replica mismatches, or deployment health problems. This will help identify if the deployment configurations are causing instability. For example, a failed rollout or scaling issue could lead to repeated pod restarts. Running `kubectl get deployments` in the namespace will reveal deployment statuses and potential errors.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 515, 'prompt_tokens': 3454, 'total_tokens': 3969, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc6d-1d1f-79d3-8c2c-22dec0f25ca5-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3454, 'output_tokens': 515, 'total_tokens': 3969}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='0386545d-b94d-4b7b-8b08-035d398c513b'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose issues in a hotel reservation application composed of many microservices. So far, they've used kubectl to list services and pods. The services list showed some Jaeger components and MongoDB instances, while the pods list indicated that most are running, but a few have restarts. The user is now looking into deployments to check for rollout issues or replica problems.\n\nThe next logical step is to check the deployments. By running `kubectl get deployments`, they can see the deployment statuses, replica counts, and any rollout errors. This will help determine if there are issues with how the applications are being deployed or scaled. For example, if a deployment has a rollout that's failed or stuck, that could be the root cause of the restarts observed in some pods. Also, checking the number of replicas and their current status can reveal if there's a mismatch that's causing instability. So, using the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool with the command `kubectl get deployments -n hotel-reservation` makes sense here. This will provide the necessary information to proceed further in diagnosing the problem.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get deployments -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8c4226a48d9948e0', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 264, 'prompt_tokens': 4477, 'total_tokens': 4741, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc6d-35b7-7e92-a6b6-7313e676ac16-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get deployments -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8c4226a48d9948e0', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4477, 'output_tokens': 264, 'total_tokens': 4741}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                     READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE   AGE\nconsul                   1/1     1            1           2m16s\nfrontend                 1/1     1            1           2m15s\ngeo                      1/1     1            1           2m15s\njaeger                   1/1     1            1           2m12s\nmemcached-profile        1/1     1            1           2m10s\nmemcached-rate           1/1     1            1           2m7s\nmemcached-reserve        1/1     1            1           2m1s\nmongodb-geo              0/1     1            0           2m14s\nmongodb-profile          1/1     1            1           2m8s\nmongodb-rate             1/1     1            1           2m6s\nmongodb-recommendation   1/1     1            1           2m4s\nmongodb-reservation      1/1     1            1           119s\nmongodb-user             1/1     1            1           112s\nprofile                  1/1     1            1           2m8s\nrate                     1/1     1            1           2m5s\nrecommendation           1/1     1            1           2m2s\nreservation              1/1     1            1           117s\nsearch                   1/1     1            1           115s\nuser                     1/1     1            1           111s\n', id='171fac05-a120-4410-b5bf-4579333fd0d9', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-8c4226a48d9948e0'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='d7265af9-5ed4-4d88-9d4c-64ccbbdebdc6'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose issues in the Hotel Reservation application, which has multiple microservices. So far, they've checked the services, pods, and deployments. The pods list showed some restarts, but the status is still Running. The deployments all look healthy with READY, UP-TO-DATE, and AVAILABLE set to 1/1. \n\nWait, but the mongodb-geo deployment is at 0/1, which means it's not available. That's a problem. The user might need to check why that deployment isn't working. The previous steps didn't show any errors in the deployments, but maybe there's an issue with the pods under that deployment.\n\nThe next step should be to look into the specific pods that are part of the mongodb-geo deployment. Since the deployment is not available, the pods might be in a CrashLoopBackOff state or another error. Using kubectl describe on the pods could provide more details. Alternatively, checking the logs of the mongodb-geo pod would help identify if there's a specific error causing it to fail.\n\nAnother possibility is that the MongoDB instance itself is having issues. Maybe the MongoDB service isn't reachable, or there's a configuration problem. The user could check the service for mongodb-geo to see if it's correctly configured. Also, looking at the metrics might show if there are any resource constraints or other anomalies.\n\nBut since the user has access to tools like get_metrics, they could query Prometheus for metrics related to mongodb-geo. For example, checking for errors or latency. Alternatively, using get_traces to see if there are any traces pointing to issues with the mongodb-geo service.\n\nHowever, the immediate next step is likely to check the specific pods under the mongodb-geo deployment. So using kubectl describe pod on mongodb-geo's pod would give more insight into why it's not running. The user might also need to check the events related to that pod to see if there are any deployment errors or resource issues.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The `mongodb-geo` deployment shows `0/1` ready replicas, indicating a critical issue. To diagnose this, I need to inspect the specific pod(s) associated with this deployment. Running `kubectl describe pod` for the `mongodb-geo` pod will reveal detailed status, events, and logs that explain why the pod is not in a healthy state (e.g., crashes, configuration errors, resource limits). This will pinpoint the root cause of the deployment failure.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': Usage(completion_tokens=524, prompt_tokens=4063, total_tokens=4587, completion_tokens_details=None, prompt_tokens_details=None), 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc6d-453b-7d81-a070-7118d8f71195-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4063, 'output_tokens': 524, 'total_tokens': 4587}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='abda1109-225e-4759-9206-6ee5e6e74be9')]]
