are restarting. For example, if the deployment has a bad image or configuration, that could cause the pods to crash and restart.\n\nAnother possibility is that some services are dependent on others. For instance, the mongodb-geo pod is in ContainerCreating state. That might be a problem. But the user hasn\'t checked the deployments yet. So first, checking deployments would help understand if there are any issues with the deployment configurations. If deployments are healthy, then the next step would be to look into the specific services that are restarting, maybe check their logs or metrics. But since the user has a tool to get metrics, maybe they can check for errors or high latency. However, the immediate next step is to check deployments to see if there\'s a rollout issue or replica problems.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The pod list shows some pods with restarts (e.g., `geo`, `profile`, `rate`, etc.), but their status is still "Running." To investigate deeper, I need to check the deployments to see if there are rollout issues, replica mismatches, or deployment health problems. This will help identify if the deployment configurations are causing instability. For example, a failed rollout or scaling issue could lead to repeated pod restarts. Running `kubectl get deployments` in the namespace will reveal deployment statuses and potential errors.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 515, 'prompt_tokens': 3454, 'total_tokens': 3969, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc6d-1d1f-79d3-8c2c-22dec0f25ca5-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3454, 'output_tokens': 515, 'total_tokens': 3969}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='0386545d-b94d-4b7b-8b08-035d398c513b'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose issues in a hotel reservation application composed of many microservices. So far, they've used kubectl to list services and pods. The services list showed some Jaeger components and MongoDB instances, while the pods list indicated that most are running, but a few have restarts. The user is now looking into deployments to check for rollout issues or replica problems.\n\nThe next logical step is to check the deployments. By running `kubectl get deployments`, they can see the deployment statuses, replica counts, and any rollout errors. This will help determine if there are issues with how the applications are being deployed or scaled. For example, if a deployment has a rollout that's failed or stuck, that could be the root cause of the restarts observed in some pods. Also, checking the number of replicas and their current status can reveal if there's a mismatch that's causing instability. So, using the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool with the command `kubectl get deployments -n hotel-reservation` makes sense here. This will provide the necessary information to proceed further in diagnosing the problem.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get deployments -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8c4226a48d9948e0', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 264, 'prompt_tokens': 4477, 'total_tokens': 4741, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc6d-35b7-7e92-a6b6-7313e676ac16-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get deployments -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-8c4226a48d9948e0', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4477, 'output_tokens': 264, 'total_tokens': 4741}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                     READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE   AGE\nconsul                   1/1     1            1           2m16s\nfrontend                 1/1     1            1           2m15s\ngeo                      1/1     1            1           2m15s\njaeger                   1/1     1            1           2m12s\nmemcached-profile        1/1     1            1           2m10s\nmemcached-rate           1/1     1            1           2m7s\nmemcached-reserve        1/1     1            1           2m1s\nmongodb-geo              0/1     1            0           2m14s\nmongodb-profile          1/1     1            1           2m8s\nmongodb-rate             1/1     1            1           2m6s\nmongodb-recommendation   1/1     1            1           2m4s\nmongodb-reservation      1/1     1            1           119s\nmongodb-user             1/1     1            1           112s\nprofile                  1/1     1            1           2m8s\nrate                     1/1     1            1           2m5s\nrecommendation           1/1     1            1           2m2s\nreservation              1/1     1            1           117s\nsearch                   1/1     1            1           115s\nuser                     1/1     1            1           111s\n', id='171fac05-a120-4410-b5bf-4579333fd0d9', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-8c4226a48d9948e0'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='d7265af9-5ed4-4d88-9d4c-64ccbbdebdc6'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose issues in the Hotel Reservation application, which has multiple microservices. So far, they've checked the services, pods, and deployments. The pods list showed some restarts, but the status is still Running. The deployments all look healthy with READY, UP-TO-DATE, and AVAILABLE set to 1/1. \n\nWait, but the mongodb-geo deployment is at 0/1, which means it's not available. That's a problem. The user might need to check why that deployment isn't working. The previous steps didn't show any errors in the deployments, but maybe there's an issue with the pods under that deployment.\n\nThe next step should be to look into the specific pods that are part of the mongodb-geo deployment. Since the deployment is not available, the pods might be in a CrashLoopBackOff state or another error. Using kubectl describe on the pods could provide more details. Alternatively, checking the logs of the mongodb-geo pod would help identify if there's a specific error causing it to fail.\n\nAnother possibility is that the MongoDB instance itself is having issues. Maybe the MongoDB service isn't reachable, or there's a configuration problem. The user could check the service for mongodb-geo to see if it's correctly configured. Also, looking at the metrics might show if there are any resource constraints or other anomalies.\n\nBut since the user has access to tools like get_metrics, they could query Prometheus for metrics related to mongodb-geo. For example, checking for errors or latency. Alternatively, using get_traces to see if there are any traces pointing to issues with the mongodb-geo service.\n\nHowever, the immediate next step is likely to check the specific pods under the mongodb-geo deployment. So using kubectl describe pod on mongodb-geo's pod would give more insight into why it's not running. The user might also need to check the events related to that pod to see if there are any deployment errors or resource issues.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The `mongodb-geo` deployment shows `0/1` ready replicas, indicating a critical issue. To diagnose this, I need to inspect the specific pod(s) associated with this deployment. Running `kubectl describe pod` for the `mongodb-geo` pod will reveal detailed status, events, and logs that explain why the pod is not in a healthy state (e.g., crashes, configuration errors, resource limits). This will pinpoint the root cause of the deployment failure.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 524, 'prompt_tokens': 4063, 'total_tokens': 4587, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc6d-453b-7d81-a070-7118d8f71195-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4063, 'output_tokens': 524, 'total_tokens': 4587}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='abda1109-225e-4759-9206-6ee5e6e74be9'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose an issue with their hotel reservation application, which consists of many microservices. So far, they've checked the services, pods, and deployments. The key issue found is the mongodb-geo deployment has 0/1 ready replicas. That means the pod isn't running properly.\n\nThe next step is to get more details about the mongodb-geo pod. The user used kubectl get deployments and saw that mongodb-geo is not ready. Now, they need to check the specific pod. The command would be kubectl describe pod for the mongodb-geo pod. But first, they need the pod's name. Wait, in the previous output, when they ran kubectl get pods, the mongodb-geo pod was listed as mongodb-geo-8cb7d9f5b-9bzrw, which is in ContainerCreating state. So the pod name is mongodb-geo-8cb7d9f5b-9bzrw.\n\nBut maybe the user hasn't checked the pod's details yet. The next logical step is to describe the pod to see events and logs. Alternatively, they could check the logs directly with kubectl logs. However, since the pod is in ContainerCreating state, it might be due to an issue during startup, like image pull errors, configuration problems, or resource constraints. Describing the pod would show events that explain why it's not running. So the best tool here is exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command kubectl describe pod mongodb-geo-8cb7d9f5b-9bzrw -n hotel-reservation. That should give detailed information about the pod's status and any errors encountered.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl describe pod mongodb-geo-8cb7d9f5b-9bzrw -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-865c9f383389eee1', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 400, 'prompt_tokens': 5077, 'total_tokens': 5477, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc6d-5dc3-7452-b0c2-d84e38275bc9-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl describe pod mongodb-geo-8cb7d9f5b-9bzrw -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-865c9f383389eee1', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5077, 'output_tokens': 400, 'total_tokens': 5477}), ToolMessage(content='Name:             mongodb-geo-8cb7d9f5b-9bzrw\nNamespace:        hotel-reservation\nPriority:         0\nService Account:  default\nNode:             node2.htliang2-agent.insai-pg0.apt.emulab.net/128.110.96.35\nStart Time:       Tue, 17 Mar 2026 15:31:35 +0000\nLabels:           io.kompose.service=mongodb-geo\n                  pod-template-hash=8cb7d9f5b\nAnnotations:      kompose.cmd: kompose convert\n                  kompose.version: 1.22.0 (955b78124)\n                  sidecar.istio.io/statsInclusionPrefixes:\n                    cluster.outbound,cluster_manager,listener_manager,http_mixer_filter,tcp_mixer_filter,server,cluster.xds-grp,listener,connection_manager\n                  sidecar.istio.io/statsInclusionRegexps: http.*\nStatus:           Pending\nIP:               \nIPs:              <none>\nControlled By:    ReplicaSet/mongodb-geo-8cb7d9f5b\nContainers:\n  hotel-reserv-geo-mongo:\n    Container ID:  \n    Image:         mongo:4.4.6\n    Image ID:      \n    Port:          27017/TCP\n    Host Port:     0/TCP\n    Args:\n      --auth\n    State:          Waiting\n      Reason:       ContainerCreating\n    Ready:          False\n    Restart Count:  0\n    Limits:\n      cpu:  1\n    Requests:\n      cpu:        100m\n    Environment:  <none>\n    Mounts:\n      /data/db from geo (rw)\n      /docker-entrypoint-initdb.d from init-script (rw)\n      /scripts from failure-script (rw)\n      /var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount from kube-api-access-6vdlh (ro)\nConditions:\n  Type                        Status\n  PodReadyToStartContainers   False \n  Initialized                 True \n  Ready                       False \n  ContainersReady             False \n  PodScheduled                True \nVolumes:\n  geo:\n    Type:       PersistentVolumeClaim (a reference to a PersistentVolumeClaim in the same namespace)\n    ClaimName:  geo-pvc\n    ReadOnly:   false\n  init-script:\n    Type:      ConfigMap (a volume populated by a ConfigMap)\n    Name:      mongo-geo-script\n    Optional:  false\n  failure-script:\n    Type:      ConfigMap (a volume populated by a ConfigMap)\n    Name:      failure-admin-geo\n    Optional:  false\n  kube-api-access-6vdlh:\n    Type:                    Projected (a volume that contains injected data from multiple sources)\n    TokenExpirationSeconds:  3607\n    ConfigMapName:           kube-root-ca.crt\n    Optional:                false\n    DownwardAPI:             true\nQoS Class:                   Burstable\nNode-Selectors:              <none>\nTolerations:                 node.kubernetes.io/not-ready:NoExecute op=Exists for 300s\n                             node.kubernetes.io/unreachable:NoExecute op=Exists for 300s\nEvents:\n  Type     Reason       Age                From               Message\n  ----     ------       ----               ----               -------\n  Normal   Scheduled    62s                default-scheduler  Successfully assigned hotel-reservation/mongodb-geo-8cb7d9f5b-9bzrw to node2.htliang2-agent.insai-pg0.apt.emulab.net\n  Warning  FailedMount  30s (x7 over 62s)  kubelet            MountVolume.SetUp failed for volume "init-script" : configmap "mongo-geo-script" not found\n', id='97f6934a-d994-4023-8ebd-df3c504cd50a', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-865c9f383389eee1'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='168f436c-4b29-473f-b837-a4988b0974e7'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose why the mongodb-geo pod is in a Pending state. From the previous steps, we know that the deployment for mongodb-geo has 0/1 replicas ready, and the pod is stuck in Pending. The describe pod output shows that the issue is with the init-script volume, specifically the configmap "mongo-geo-script" not being found.\n\nSo, the next step is to check if the ConfigMap named "mongo-geo-script" exists in the hotel-reservation namespace. If it\'s missing, that would explain why the mount is failing. The user might have forgotten to create the ConfigMap, or it might have been deleted. \n\nThe tool to use here is exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd. The command would be to get the ConfigMap using kubectl get configmap mongo-geo-script -n hotel-reservation. If the ConfigMap doesn\'t exist, that\'s the root cause. Alternatively, if it does exist, maybe there\'s a typo in the name. \n\nAnother possibility is that the ConfigMap was created but in a different namespace. But since the pod is in the hotel-reservation namespace, the ConfigMap should also be there. So checking its existence is the first step. If it\'s not there, the solution would be to create it. If it is there, maybe the pod needs to be restarted or there\'s another issue. \n\nSo the tool call should be to check the existence of the ConfigMap. That\'s the logical next step to confirm the missing ConfigMap.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The `mongodb-geo` pod is failing due to a missing ConfigMap `mongo-geo-script` as indicated by the `FailedMount` event. To confirm this, I need to check if the ConfigMap exists in the `hotel-reservation` namespace. If it is missing, this would explain the mount failure and is a clear root cause. Running `kubectl get configmap mongo-geo-script -n hotel-reservation` will verify its presence.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 427, 'prompt_tokens': 5115, 'total_tokens': 5542, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc6d-7398-7783-a05e-366ac5927156-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 5115, 'output_tokens': 427, 'total_tokens': 5542}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='3c55e30c-dde2-4515-991b-d54d716047f3'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose why the mongodb-geo pod is not starting. From the previous steps, they checked the pods and deployments, and found that mongodb-geo has 0/1 ready replicas. When they described the pod, they saw that the issue is a FailedMount event because the ConfigMap "mongo-geo-script" is not found.\n\nSo the next logical step is to check if that ConfigMap actually exists in the namespace. The user needs to run a command to list all ConfigMaps in the hotel-reservation namespace to see if "mongo-geo-script" is present. Alternatively, they could try to get the specific ConfigMap directly. But since the error says it\'s not found, maybe the ConfigMap wasn\'t created properly, or maybe there\'s a typo in the name. \n\nThe available tool is exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd. The command should be something like "kubectl get configmap mongo-geo-script -n hotel-reservation" to check for the existence of that specific ConfigMap. If it\'s not there, that\'s the root cause. If it is there, maybe there\'s another issue. But given the error message, the ConfigMap is missing, so this command will confirm that. \n\nSo the tool call should be to execute "kubectl get configmap mongo-geo-script -n hotel-reservation" to verify if the ConfigMap exists. If it doesn\'t, then the root cause is that the ConfigMap is missing, which is preventing the pod from mounting the volume and starting.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get configmap mongo-geo-script -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-ad0ee069f27f8eee', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 355, 'prompt_tokens': 6125, 'total_tokens': 6480, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc6d-87fb-7b42-bba6-34fb8bc5b93a-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get configmap mongo-geo-script -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-ad0ee069f27f8eee', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 6125, 'output_tokens': 355, 'total_tokens': 6480}), ToolMessage(content='Command Rejected: Error executing kubectl command:\nError from server (NotFound): configmaps "mongo-geo-script" not found\n', id='2e0baf9b-cf73-44db-8b3b-d8507cc1d9c6', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-ad0ee069f27f8eee'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='e9197c25-5928-46b3-ad5e-a035a00bfa58')]]
